==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*bindaną}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*bʰendʰ-||bind}}.  Cognate with {{etyl|osx|-}} {{m|osx|bindan}} ({{etyl|nds|-}} {{m|nds|binnen}}), {{etyl|odt|-}} {{m|odt|bindan}} ({{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|binden}}), {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|bintan}} ({{etyl|de|-}} {{m|de|binden}}), {{etyl|non|-}} {{m|non|binda}} ({{etyl|da|-}} {{m|da|binde}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{m|sv|binda}}), {{etyl|got|-}} {{m|got|𐌱𐌹𐌽𐌳𐌰𐌽}}.  The Indo-European root was also the source of Avestan ''bendayaiti'' and Greek {{l/grc|πεῖσμα}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈbindɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb}}

# to [[bind]], [[constrain]]
# to [[fasten]] or [[join]]

====Conjugation====
{{ang-conj-strong|class=3|bind|band|bund|bund}}

====Descendants====
* Middle English: {{l|enm|binden}}
** English: [[bind]]

